# What each document says about couplings, and whether it says anything at all. One row per
# filing in the chosen scope: how many couplings it states, and what its coupling search
# reported if it states none.
#
# Two columns, two facts
#   An empty coupling table carries only the first. A filing with no coupling rows is a stack of
#   independent layers or a document where nobody looked, and which one it is has never been in
#   the coupling table. It is in the search beside it, which is why that element had to exist.
#
# The scope is a slot
#   The two callers genuinely disagree, and both are right. `rules.sql` asks this of the corpus:
#   "has anybody tested the independence assumption" is a claim about the evidence, and a fixture
#   that asserts independence on purpose would answer it with a yes nobody earned. `matrices.sql`
#   asks it of every filing, because it is a tour of what the matrix cannot express and the
#   stipulations demonstrate that best. Written into each of those files instead, it is one
#   relation with identical joins and two scopes. That is how two copies of one relation start.
-- pm:Stack/pm:couplings against pm:couplings/pm:absent, over a scope the caller names.
SELECT f.filing,
       count(c.filing) AS couplings_filed,
       s.answer        AS search_answer
FROM      (
    :compose(@scope)
) f
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(entries/couplings.sqlc)
) c USING (filing)
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(epistemics/coupling_searches.sqlc)
) s ON s.filing = f.filing
GROUP BY f.filing, s.answer
